Grade 1 Lateral Ankle Sprain
Common Infections and Afflictions
Mild pain over ATFL, Intact joint stability, Full weight-bearing ability

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-015
Reason for Visit: Acute right ankle injury with pain and swelling following a misstep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acute inversion injury to the right ankle sustained two hours prior, accompanied by immediate pain and reluctance to bear full weight out of fear of structural damage. Given the acute mechanism of injury and the potential for an occult malleolar or midfoot fracture, an urgent on-site clinical assessment is required to perform a structured physical examination applying the Ottawa Ankle Rules. This definitive assessment determines the immediate necessity for emergency radiological imaging versus conservative home management. Delaying this assessment poses a risk of mobilizing a potential unstable fracture or causing unnecessary distress and an unwarranted emergency department presentation, strictly fulfilling the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: I rolled my ankle stepping off the curb, and it's throbbing right on the outside bone.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 2 hours ago following an inversion injury to the right ankle. The patient noted immediate, mild localized pain over the anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) region. The patient was able to bear weight immediately post-injury and ambulate home but remains highly anxious about a hidden fracture. Reports minimal functional loss but experiences mild discomfort with inversion of the ankle. Denies a distinct "pop" or "snap" sensation, mechanical locking, or distal numbness.
Functional Status: Ambulatory with a cautious, antalgic gait.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives in a two-story home. Minor risk regarding stair navigation.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 122/78 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, resting with the right leg elevated. Mild distress consistent with acute soft tissue injury.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Negative plain radiographs of the ankle ruling out fracture according to the Ottawa Ankle Rules would assist in confirming the diagnosis, but are clinically not indicated based on current physical findings.
Physical Exam: Right ankle demonstrates minimal to absent localized swelling over the lateral aspect. No significant ecchymosis observed. Point tenderness is elicited strictly over the lateral ankle ligaments (ATFL). Normal bony contours are palpated without any point tenderness on the posterior edge or tip of the lateral malleolus, medial malleolus, base of the fifth metatarsal, or navicular bone. The patient is able to bear weight and take 4 consecutive steps independently without severe pain. Intact ankle joint stability is confirmed on the anterior drawer test with a firm endpoint. Neurovascular status is intact distally.
Environmental Observations: Residence is secure. Stairs possess sturdy handrails.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient applied a bag of frozen peas for 10 minutes prior to arrival.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 Lateral Ankle Sprain - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Lateral Malleolus Fracture - Ruled out clinically by the strict application of the Ottawa Ankle Rules; patient lacks bony point tenderness over the malleolar zone and is capable of independent weight-bearing for 4 steps.
Syndesmotic (High) Ankle Sprain - Unlikely given the mechanism of injury (inversion rather than forced external rotation/dorsiflexion) and the absence of tenderness over the anterior inferior tibiofibular ligament (AITFL).
Achilles Tendon Rupture - Ruled out by a negative Thompson test, intact active plantarflexion, and the localized anterior/lateral pain pattern.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute inversion injury and subsequent localized pain necessitated urgent evaluation to clinically clear the joint of a fracture and avoid unnecessary emergency department crowding. By applying the validated Ottawa Ankle Rules in the home, a malleolar or midfoot fracture was definitively ruled out clinically. The physical exam confirmed a stable, minor ligamentous stretch (Grade 1 Sprain). Providing immediate reassurance, initiating a structured functional rehabilitation plan, and avoiding an unnecessary, costly, and time-consuming after-hours x-ray trip directly fulfills the mandate for an urgent after-hours MBS 585-600 intervention to prevent patient deterioration and distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Advised Paracetamol 1000mg strictly every 6 hours and Ibuprofen 400mg three times daily with food for the next 48 hours for analgesia and anti-inflammatory action. Instructed on the RICE protocol (Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation) with an emphasis on early, protected mobilization.
Positive Behavioral Change: To accelerate healing, trace the letters of the alphabet in the air with your big toe 3 times a day. This specific active range-of-motion exercise pumps out accumulated swelling and aligns the newly forming collagen fibers in the ligament, physically preventing the ankle from healing stiff and weak.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Applied a simple tubular compression bandage (Tubigrip) for symptomatic support.
Follow-up: Review with a Physiotherapist in 5-7 days for structured strengthening and proprioceptive training.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department or Urgent Care immediately if the foot becomes completely numb, turns cold and pale, or if you become entirely unable to put any weight on the leg.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Soft tissue diagnosis. If pain fails to improve after 7-10 days, a delayed x-ray or ultrasound may be indicated to evaluate for occult bony injury or higher-grade ligamentous tearing.
Clinical Course: Grade 1 sprains typically recover rapidly. Expect full, pain-free functional return within 1 to 3 weeks with diligent, early mobilization.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek medical review if severe swelling suddenly develops, or if the pain dramatically worsens despite regular pain relief.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: You have stretched, but not torn, the primary stabilizing ligament on the outside of your ankle. Because the joint is perfectly stable, completely immobilizing it will actually slow down your recovery.
Actionable Steps: Walk as normally as pain allows. When icing, place a damp cloth between the ice pack and your skin, and strictly limit application to 15-20 minutes every 2 hours to prevent frostbite and rebound swelling.

